From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grigory Evko <grigory@evko.io>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 10:13:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] c++: plugin events for folded initializers and empty-class
 assignments

A plugin that checks C++ source reads the semantic tree at
PLUGIN_PRE_GENERICIZE.  The front end changes two kinds of tree before that
event, and a fact of the source is then lost.

First, the front end replaces an initializer with the tree that a fold
gives.  When the value of the initializer is a constant, the fold drops
each call, conversion and std::move in it.  This occurs, for example, for
a call of a constexpr function that takes an empty class by reference: the
tree keeps an empty CONSTRUCTOR and no sign of the call.  The front end
folds at four places: store_init_value for the initializer of a variable,
expand_default_init for a call of a constexpr constructor, massage_init_elt
for an element of a braced list, and build_vec_init for an element of an
array.

Second, build_over_call replaces a call of a trivial assignment operator of
an empty class with COMPOUND_EXPR (SOURCE, TARGET).  In an expression
statement, convert_to_void then replaces TARGET with a void constant, so the
tree does not show which object the assignment assigns to.

Two new events give these facts to a plugin while the front end makes the
change.  PLUGIN_FOLDED_INITIALIZER gives the kind of the place, the object,
the initializer before the fold, the tree that replaces it, and the
location, each time that the fold gives a different tree.
PLUGIN_EMPTY_CLASS_ASSIGNMENT gives the target, the source, the assignment
operator and the location.  The source stays in the tree as the first
operand of the COMPOUND_EXPR, so a plugin can find the place of the
assignment.  The two events also come at the instantiation of a template.

The output of the compiler does not change.  Each event goes through
invoke_plugin_callbacks, so with no plugin the cost is one load and one
branch when a fold gives a different tree, and nothing more.  On three
heavy units of a C++26 code base, the user instructions of cc1plus with
-fsyntax-only and no plugin change by less than 0.01 %, less than the
spread of three runs.  The units give approximately 10000 events each, and
a callback that counts them costs less than the spread too.

gcc/ChangeLog:

	* plugin.def (PLUGIN_FOLDED_INITIALIZER)
	(PLUGIN_EMPTY_CLASS_ASSIGNMENT): New events.
	* plugin.cc (register_callback, invoke_plugin_callbacks_full): Handle
	the new events.
	* doc/plugins.texi (Plugin callbacks): Document the new events.

gcc/cp/ChangeLog:

	* cp-tree.h (enum plugin_folded_initializer_kind)
	(struct plugin_folded_initializer_data)
	(struct plugin_empty_class_assignment_data): New.
	* typeck2.cc: Include plugin.h.
	(store_init_value): Invoke PLUGIN_FOLDED_INITIALIZER when the folds
	give a different initializer.
	(massage_init_elt): Invoke PLUGIN_FOLDED_INITIALIZER when the fold of
	an element gives a different constant.
	* init.cc: Include plugin.h.
	(expand_default_init): Invoke PLUGIN_FOLDED_INITIALIZER when the call
	of a constexpr constructor folds to a constant.
	(build_vec_init): Invoke PLUGIN_FOLDED_INITIALIZER when the
	initialization of an element folds to a different constant.
	* call.cc: Include plugin.h.
	(build_over_call): Invoke PLUGIN_EMPTY_CLASS_ASSIGNMENT for a trivial
	assignment of an empty class.

gcc/testsuite/ChangeLog:

	* g++.dg/plugin/plugin.exp: Add the two tests that follow.
	* g++.dg/plugin/folded_initializer_plugin.cc: New test plugin.
	* g++.dg/plugin/folded-initializer-plugin-test.C: New test.
	* g++.dg/plugin/empty_class_assignment_plugin.cc: New test plugin.
	* g++.dg/plugin/empty-class-assignment-plugin-test.C: New test.
